WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.840
All right, my friends.

00:00.840 --> 00:06.920
So now we can learn how to modify the structure of our database by using commands of the data definition

00:06.920 --> 00:08.040
language DDL.

00:08.280 --> 00:12.840
So we're going to learn how to create a new table, update the content of a table and as well how to

00:12.880 --> 00:13.800
drop a table.

00:13.840 --> 00:14.760
So let's go.

00:18.760 --> 00:19.080
Okay.

00:19.120 --> 00:24.000
So usually if you have like an empty database, what you want to do is to go and define the structure

00:24.000 --> 00:24.800
of your data.

00:24.800 --> 00:29.120
So one of the first things that we usually do is we go and create new tables.

00:29.120 --> 00:31.320
So here we have a command called create.

00:31.320 --> 00:36.320
And if you use it you can create a new object inside the database like for example a table.

00:36.320 --> 00:39.040
So once you execute it you're going to get the brand new table.

00:39.080 --> 00:42.000
And usually the table is going to be empty without any data.

00:42.000 --> 00:43.200
So it is very simple.

00:43.200 --> 00:45.280
This is what the create command does.

00:45.320 --> 00:48.200
And now let's go to SQL in order to create a new table.

00:48.200 --> 00:49.960
So my friends we have the following task.

00:49.960 --> 00:56.400
Create a new table called persons with columns ID, person, name, birth date and phone.

00:56.440 --> 00:59.000
Okay, so this time we will not start by select.

00:59.000 --> 01:01.800
We will start with the command create table.

01:02.200 --> 01:07.070
So we are telling SQL to create a table, and after that we have to define the name of the table.

01:07.070 --> 01:09.190
So in this task we have to call it persons.

01:09.230 --> 01:11.910
Now we have to go and open two parentheses like this.

01:11.910 --> 01:14.870
And in between we have to define the columns.

01:14.870 --> 01:15.590
So what do we need.

01:15.630 --> 01:16.990
First we need an ID.

01:17.030 --> 01:18.630
So this is the first column name.

01:18.630 --> 01:21.390
And next we have to define which data type for this column.

01:21.390 --> 01:22.790
It's going to be an int.

01:22.790 --> 01:25.310
So it is a number does not contain any characters.

01:25.350 --> 01:27.470
And now next we can define some constraints.

01:27.470 --> 01:29.350
And we cannot have a person without an ID.

01:29.350 --> 01:31.070
So it should not be null.

01:31.070 --> 01:31.710
So not null.

01:31.710 --> 01:32.910
This is the first column.

01:32.910 --> 01:36.950
So we have defined the name of the column the data type and the constraint okay.

01:36.990 --> 01:38.230
So let's go to the second column.

01:38.230 --> 01:39.350
And here we're going to have a comma.

01:39.350 --> 01:43.310
And the next one name going to be person name.

01:43.310 --> 01:44.590
So this is the column name.

01:44.630 --> 01:45.910
And the person name we can have.

01:45.950 --> 01:50.950
And now the data type for this column is going to be a varchar because the person name contains characters.

01:50.950 --> 01:52.270
So varchar.

01:52.310 --> 01:53.950
And now we have to define the length.

01:53.950 --> 01:56.230
So I'm going to go with 50 characters.

01:56.270 --> 01:57.990
And now I would say this is a must.

01:57.990 --> 01:59.750
So each person should have a name.

01:59.750 --> 02:01.790
So I'm going to say not null as well.

02:01.790 --> 02:05.110
So that we have the name the type and the constraint.

02:05.150 --> 02:08.770
Now let's move to the third column is going to be birth dates.

02:08.810 --> 02:12.450
Now which type of informations we have inside the birth dates.

02:12.450 --> 02:15.330
So it's going to be a date, not a number not characters.

02:15.330 --> 02:16.850
So we're going to go with the data type date.

02:16.890 --> 02:18.250
And now about the constraint.

02:18.250 --> 02:24.050
Well depends I would say in our application it is an optional because this is very personal information.

02:24.050 --> 02:26.890
And maybe some persons will not provide their birth dates.

02:26.890 --> 02:29.970
So this is an optional and I will not say it is not null.

02:29.970 --> 02:31.210
So nulls are allowed.

02:31.250 --> 02:32.490
Now let's move on to the next one.

02:32.490 --> 02:33.970
It's going to be the phone.

02:33.970 --> 02:35.890
So now what is the data type of a phone.

02:35.890 --> 02:37.770
Well we have some types numbers.

02:37.770 --> 02:40.050
We have characters special characters.

02:40.050 --> 02:41.330
So we could have anything.

02:41.330 --> 02:43.170
So that's why I'm going to go with the Varchar.

02:43.210 --> 02:46.930
And here you can specify the length that you think it's okay I'm going to go with 15.

02:46.970 --> 02:49.490
Now of course depend on the system that you are building.

02:49.490 --> 02:55.130
I would say the fonts are very important in order to validate whether this is a real person.

02:55.130 --> 02:57.250
So we're going to say not null.

02:57.290 --> 02:59.930
So we are not allowing nulls in this field.

02:59.970 --> 03:00.410
Perfect.

03:00.410 --> 03:03.250
So with that we have covered all the columns that are required.

03:03.250 --> 03:06.250
We have defined the data types and as well the constraints.

03:06.290 --> 03:11.680
Now the last thing in each database table we should Richard has a primary key in order to make sure

03:11.680 --> 03:15.800
this table has an integrity and maybe as well connectable to other tables.

03:15.840 --> 03:20.760
So now what we're going to do, we're going to go and add the primary key constraint for the last column.

03:20.760 --> 03:22.400
And then we're going to say constraints.

03:22.440 --> 03:24.640
Now we have to give a primary key name.

03:24.680 --> 03:27.000
This is only going to be visible for the database.

03:27.000 --> 03:29.400
So I'm going to call it PK for primary key.

03:29.480 --> 03:30.880
And here persons.

03:30.880 --> 03:33.960
And then after that we're going to say primary key.

03:34.000 --> 03:37.960
And between two parentheses we're going to go and pick which one is the primary key.

03:38.000 --> 03:39.960
And of course it's going to be the ID.

03:39.960 --> 03:41.800
So we can go over here and say ID.

03:41.800 --> 03:44.080
So again we are saying there is a new constraint.

03:44.080 --> 03:45.600
This is the name of it.

03:45.640 --> 03:47.240
It's only internal for the database.

03:47.240 --> 03:51.200
And then we are saying this one is a primary key on the field ID.

03:51.200 --> 03:52.080
So that's it.

03:52.120 --> 03:54.520
With that we have defined a primary key for our table.

03:54.560 --> 03:56.280
Let's go and execute it.

03:56.280 --> 03:57.840
So as you can see it is successful.

03:57.840 --> 04:00.360
Let's go and check our database for our new table.

04:00.360 --> 04:04.880
So if you don't see it already you have to right click on the database and then go and refresh.

04:04.880 --> 04:06.160
So let's go to the tables.

04:06.200 --> 04:09.080
And now we have a brand new table called persons.

04:09.080 --> 04:11.280
So with that we have created our new table.

04:11.320 --> 04:15.430
Now of course for the DDL commands you will not get results or data.

04:15.470 --> 04:20.830
All what you are getting is a message from the database and the message says here the command completed

04:20.830 --> 04:23.550
successfully and then we have a date when this is completed.

04:23.550 --> 04:26.790
So that means that DDL commands will never return data.

04:26.830 --> 04:29.790
It is changing the structure of your database.

04:29.830 --> 04:32.350
It's not about retrieving any data and so on.

04:32.350 --> 04:35.270
So this command did change something in our database.

04:35.270 --> 04:37.510
And in this scenario it created a new table.

04:37.510 --> 04:43.630
And that's why we call this data definition language DDL because we are defining the database.

04:43.670 --> 04:48.630
Now of course if you go and say select star from our new table persons.

04:48.630 --> 04:51.550
So let's go and highlight it and then execute it.

04:51.550 --> 04:54.190
You will see we are getting of course the columns.

04:54.190 --> 04:57.230
So the ID, the person name birth date, the phone.

04:57.230 --> 04:58.670
But we don't have any rows.

04:58.710 --> 05:00.790
That means our table is empty.

05:00.830 --> 05:06.910
Now what is very important is that you go and save those informations in an SQL script, because maybe

05:06.910 --> 05:09.070
later you have to redefine this table.

05:09.070 --> 05:12.710
But let's say that you have created different queries and you have lost the scripts.

05:12.750 --> 05:16.650
And now I would like to see again the create statements for this table.

05:16.650 --> 05:18.010
Well, there is trick for that.

05:18.010 --> 05:22.010
If you go to the left side you see the persons right here, right click on it.

05:22.010 --> 05:24.570
And then you have here script table as.

05:24.610 --> 05:27.810
And now we have here different options that you can run on the table.

05:27.810 --> 05:30.210
And the first one says create two.

05:30.250 --> 05:32.690
Then let's go to New Query editor.

05:33.730 --> 05:34.530
So now what happened.

05:34.530 --> 05:40.530
The database did read the metadata information about the person and created your DDL query with many

05:40.530 --> 05:41.930
extra stuff that we haven't done.

05:41.970 --> 05:44.610
But this is the template that the database uses.

05:44.610 --> 05:46.050
So now we can see a lot of stuff.

05:46.050 --> 05:48.930
But what is interesting is this create table.

05:48.930 --> 05:52.810
So we can see create table the schema dbo the default one.

05:52.810 --> 05:53.770
Then the persons.

05:53.810 --> 05:58.250
And then we have our columns the data type and as well the constraints.

05:58.250 --> 06:03.250
So with that you got back your DDL statements and many other stuff about the table which is now not

06:03.250 --> 06:03.930
interesting.

06:03.930 --> 06:08.210
But now what I really need is to see the create statements about this table.

06:08.210 --> 06:10.370
So this is how you can get back your DDL command.

06:10.370 --> 06:15.810
But of course what I recommend you is always put your code inside a git repository and always keep it

06:15.810 --> 06:19.130
up to date so that always you can check your work and extend it.
